THE DIVERGENCE (already diagnosed and measured -- do not re-investigate it):
The C header c_src/src/mdmacros.h declares two MUTABLE globals:
    extern int (*G_OP)(int, int);
    extern const char *G_OP_NAME;   /* the pointer itself is non-const */
and c_src/src/mdcore.c defines them as
    int (*G_OP)(int,int) = OP_FN(OP);
    const char *G_OP_NAME = STR(OP);
In the C .so these land in the writable `.data` section, so an external consumer that does `*(int(**)(int,int))dlsym(h,"G_OP") = some_fn;` succeeds.

The current Rust translation in src/mdcore.rs uses immutable `static`s:
    #[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub static G_OP: OpFn = mdmacros::OP_FN;
    #[repr(transparent)] pub struct CStrPtr(pub *const c_char);
    unsafe impl Sync for CStrPtr {}
    #[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub static G_OP_NAME: CStrPtr = CStrPtr(mdmacros::OP_NAME.as_ptr() as *const c_char);
rustc places immutable statics in `.data.rel.ro`, which the dynamic loader maps READ-ONLY after relocation (PT_GNU_RELRO). A consumer store therefore SIGSEGVs against the Rust .so while it succeeds against the C .so. This was confirmed empirically (C: rc=0, Rust: rc=139 segfault).

